Make xmalloc_substitute_string handle an empty search string.
count_strstr already counts an empty string as matching before every
character and at the end, but xmalloc_substitute_string kept finding
the empty string at the same place, appending the replacement forever
and writing past the buffer sized by that count. "xargs -I ''" passes
the user's string straight through and crashed on the first input
line.

Copy one character past each empty match, and stop after the match at
the end of the string, so the result agrees with count_strstr.

---
 libbb/replace.c |    7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

--- a/libbb/replace.c
+++ b/libbb/replace.c
@@ -42,6 +42,13 @@
 		dst = mempcpy(dst, repl, repl_len);
 		/*src = end + 1; - GNU findutils 4.5.10 doesn't do this... */
 		src = end + sub_len; /* but this. Try "xargs -Iaa echo aaa" */
+		if (sub_len == 0) {
+			/* Empty sub matches before every char and at the end,
+			 * as count_strstr() counts it */
+			if (*src == '\0')
+				break;
+			*dst++ = *src++;
+		}
 	}
 	strcpy(dst, src);
 	//dbg_msg("subst9:'%s'", buf);
